e configuration does it need? Those details affect both the contract and the price. Making compute tradeable Rather than waiting for every GPU cluster to become interchangeable, SF Compute matches orders with different constraints. Evan argues that this makes trading possible while the technology changes, at the cost of spreading liquidity across more variants. We also discuss spot and forward pricing, batch versus live inference, and why an index can become disconnected from the cost of actually buying compute. Independence is central to Evan&#8217;s case for a market operator. He argues that companies with large hardware or inference businesses have incentives that make it harder to run a neutral exchange. We also get into the unglamorous engineering: visiting sites, designing clusters, running the backplane, and making infrastructure programmable. Near the end, he describes the ability to subdivide InfiniBand fabric on the fly as one example. Guest: Evan Conrad, CEO of SF Compute . Show notes Evan&#8217;s AI Engineer NYC talk: How Compute Markets Work Our previous SF Compute conversation Why oil futures went negative in 2020 &#8212; EIA How SF Compute customers resell unused GPU capacity SF Compute&#8217;s $245M compute sale Abel: SF Compute&#8217;s settlement ledger CFTC: Barclays and LIBOR manipulation Elastic InfiniBand partitions Timestamps 0:00 &#8212; Why can&#8217;t NVIDIA build this? 0:25 &#8212; Welcome back to Latent Space 2:23 &#8212; From selling a room to running the hotel 3:47 &#8212; Physical settlement: actually delivering compute 6:00 &#8212; Liquidity and the GPU order book 8:42 &#8212; Trading nonstandard compute 10:00 &#8212; Interrupting and moving workloads 12:20 &#8212; Why hardware cohorts matter 14:32 &#8212; How far out do compute contracts go? 16:19 &#8212; Batch versus live inference leases 17:58 &#8212; The shape of compute prices 20:33 &#8212; GPU generations, specifications and SKUs 23:30 &#8212; Physical versus cash settlement 27:0